% Alexina de Magalhães Pinto, Cantigas das creanças e do povo (1916).
% Published early from the workshop on 11 November 2026: each tune names what is guessed or left out in its N: lines,
% and its workshop record (or review item) is kept for further correction.

X:1
T:Na Bahia tem
T:In Bahia There Is
C:traditional (Brazil: Rio, Bahia, Minas Gerais)
O:Brazil (Rio de Janeiro, Bahia, Minas Gerais)
R:dança cantada (sung dance in two lines)
S:Alexina de Magalhães Pinto, Cantigas das creanças e do povo e danças populares (Rio de Janeiro: F. Alves, 1916), p. 15; the dance pp. 109-112
N:A Brazilian sung dance that children danced in Minas Gerais. The words boast of what Bahia has (a penny coconut, a glass lamp, a flat-iron, a sewing machine): Bahia was the colony's first capital, where novelties came in. The dance (Alexina, p. 112): two lines face each other about seven steps apart; bars 1-2, three steps forward, clapping once on each step; bars 3-4, three steps back; bars 5-6 forward again, 7-8 back; then partners greet and may cross over, the lines changing places, and sing it again. One line can sing the first half and the other answer. In her dance version a polka in pairs follows, for piano alone; it is left out here because the scan is too blurred to read its tune. Guessed: the two sixteenths on "Seu bem" (they may be eighths). The accents are Alexina's; the 1916 spellings are kept; the tempo is ours. The same words are also sung in Umbanda; this is the children's dance of 1916.
Q:1/4=100
M:2/4
L:1/8
K:D
